  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/7/79/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_6.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The splash page promises that the adventure offered this month will show <i>why</i> Spider-Man has \"become America's super sensation!\"\n\nA man-sized Lizard wearing pants and a lab coat appears for the first time in a swamp in the Florida Everglades and chases off what he calls \"puny humans,\" using his great strength to drive them away from the swamp.&nbsp; News about the super-strong Lizard creature quickly spreads all over the nation -- even as far North as New York City, where the <i>Daily Bugle</i> prints a headline challenging Spider-Man to defeat the Lizard!\n\nNot interested in the challenge so much as a possible trip to Florida to snap photos for the paper, Peter Parker goes to visit J. Jonah Jameson and volunteers to head down and get pictures of the Lizard.&nbsp; But Jameson admits that he only printed the challenge in the paper \"to sell papers!\"&nbsp; And he doesn't even really believe that this Lizard character is even <i>real</i>.&nbsp; Regardless, he's not going to spend money to send Parker down there so he can \"waste time.\"\n\nNevertheless, Peter learns all he can about lizards (from the Natural History Museum), and in the process, has to save Liz from a couple of crooks who tried to steal a ruby from one of its display cases, ultimately using Liz as a hostage.&nbsp; Liz is <i>thrilled</i> at being saved by Spider-Man, but it isn't getting Peter any closer to Florida.\n\nFinally, Spider-Man himself shows up at Jameson's office and <i>agrees</i> to accept the challenge, while at the same time suggesting the <i>Bugle</i> send a photographer down to Florida to cover the story.&nbsp; Sure enough, the moment Spider-Man leaves, Jameson contacts Peter Parker and surprises him by announcing that he's going along as <i>well</i>!&nbsp; It's too big of a story to trust to the young photographer.\n\nAfter getting permission from Aunt May (who only agrees to it because he's going to be accompanied by that \"nice man you do part-time work for\"), Peter meets Jameson at the airport.&nbsp; On the plane ride down, Peter educates himself with reading material about lizards, clippings about the locale this new Lizard has been spotted in, and even reads about a Dr. Curtis Connors, a reptile expert who lives near there (who he hopes can be of assistance).\n\nOnce in Florida, Peter excuses himself to buy some film supplies, then changes to Spider-Man and heads directly for the swamp where the Lizard has been seen.&nbsp; But his approach is detected by the Lizard, ambushed, and the two soon come to blows.&nbsp; During the brief battle, Spider-Man notes that he's not wearing any kind of disguise -- \"that grotesque form is really <i>him</i>!\"\n\nSpider-Man completely underestimates his strength and suddenly finds himself hurled bodily about a half mile away, where it takes him to the vicinity of Dr. Connors' home, and so he decides to pay a call there and warn them that the Lizard is nearby.&nbsp; But when he approaches and sees a woman weeping inside, he learns it is the wife of Dr. Connors.&nbsp; And in the next moment, Spider-Man is told that her husband <i>is </i>the Lizard!\n\nShe then explains how her husband sought a way to regrow his right arm that he lost during the war, being particularly fascinated by reptiles and their ability to regrow limbs.&nbsp; He soon \"became one of the world's leading authorities on reptiles.\"&nbsp; He became obsessed at the possibility of being able to introduce such an ability to the human race.\n\nConnors worked tirelessly on finding a serum, while at the same time being a good father to their son, Billy.&nbsp; After an experiment on a test rabbit produced a regrown leg, there was nothing left to do but test his formula on himself -- the first <i>human</i> subject.\n\nAnd before his wife could stop him, he drained the vial of serum.&nbsp; Moments later, he stared with delight down at his newly-regrown right hand!\n\nBut... the growth didn't end there, and his body began to mutate slowly into a human-sized <b>lizard</b>!&nbsp; Unwilling for his family to see him like this, the reptilian creature fled into the night.&nbsp; He returned the next day to try and find a cure for himself, but sensing his brain gradually affected (and dulled by the transformation), he used the last of his humanity to scribble down a note of warning to his wife.\n\n\"Take Billy... leave -- never come back -\"\n\nMartha Connors' story is interrupted by a cry for help from outside.&nbsp; <i>It's Billy</i>, who she thought was taking a nap in another room.&nbsp; Instead, he's out wandering around the swamp, finally being scared away by the sight of the Lizard and calling out.&nbsp; Spider-Man immediately races out to try and find him and encounters the Lizard for the second time, noting how <i>hard</i> his armor hide is.&nbsp; (But the Lizard seems strangely attracted to the boy.)\n\nOnce the Lizard has fled the scene, Billy recognizes Spider-Man from pictures he's seen, and soon, with their help, the science-prodigy-in-disguise asks the Connors to see the doctor's notes.&nbsp; Perhaps he can find some kind of antidote.\n\nSpider-Man works for hours for a cure to the Lizard's condition, finally believing that he's found it.&nbsp; But as he holds up the serum, he wonders \"How am I going to get him to <i>drink</i> it??\"\n\nBut as it turns out, Spider-Man at least won't have to hunt down the Lizard, because at the moment, he barges into the house, angry that this Spider-Man character doesn't fear him like all the other humans do.&nbsp; (And it's clear now that he no longer recognizes his own wife and son, becoming less human by the minute.)\n\n\n<b>FIXME - continue here</b>\n\n\n\n\nSpider-Man drives him off, but the Lizard plans to mutate ordinary lizards into a huge army to attack humans, and Spider-Man soon tracks him to a body of water where he is apparently talking to a group of alligators.&nbsp; The two engage in combat, and in the process, Spider-Man manages to dump his antidote down the Lizard's throat.&nbsp; In moments, he reverts to Dr. Connors.\n\nThey return to his home, and Connor burns his notes so that his experiment will never be repeated.&nbsp; Spider-Man agrees to keep the secret that the Lizard was Dr. Connors.&nbsp; Jameson doesn't believe that the Lizard was ever real, so he thinks the photos Peter gives him are fakes.\n\nBack home, Spider-Man sends Jameson a letter reading \"Roses are red, Violets are blue...I'm still at large, so phooey to you!\"&nbsp; Jameson swears to get him if it's the last thing he does!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "A lackluster issue, certainly the low-point so far for this mag.&nbsp; We did <i>not</i> collect on the promise made on the splash page about why Spider-Man is so popular (unless you perhaps count the \"personal message\" paragraph at the end of the Announcements section -- and perhaps its very nice message <i>does</i> qualify).\n\nThe only thing that I can think of that might have been established in this issue is the banter of Spider-Man while engaging with his enemies.&nbsp; That seemed to be prominently featured here more than before (beginning even during the short aside that featured Spider-Man taking down a couple of crooks at the National History Museum), so that's the one positive in an otherwise unmemorable outing.\n\nIt was readable, yes, but it didn't do anything to make me love this character more and seemed overall to lack the charm and \"home-iness\" of the previous issues.\n\n[ASSET: center | SpideyAndJameson | width=67%]\n\nI did enjoy the interplay between Spider-Man and Jameson.&nbsp; Spider-Man's treatment of Dr. Connors and his family could be classified as \"tender\" even, but when it comes to dealing with Jameson, who continues to try and drag Spider-Man's name through the mud every chance he gets, he has no problem webbing his mouth shut, gluing him to his chair, dangling him from ceilings, or even mailing him nasti-grams, <i>heh</i>.&nbsp; And you can't <i>blame</i> him -- this is a strength of the book and adds a little humor to a story featuring a largely uninteresting villain.\n\n\n[ASSET: left | NiceManJameson | width=12%] Another nice aside was the moment where Peter got permission to travel to Florida, receiving her approval only after he indicated that the \"nice man\" he worked for at the Bugle would be going <i>with</i> him.\n[BR]\n[BR] Peter assures his aunt that Jameson is [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"like a big, fat guardian angel, Aunt May!\"]\n[BR] (eliciting a loud guffaw from me, <i>heh</i>).\n\n\n[ASSET: right | FirstEncounter | width=12%]\n\n<b>FIXME - continue here</b>\n\n\n\n\nI have to say that the first half of the book kept me in my seat.&nbsp; Oh sure, the business about Spider-Man getting to Florida in the first place and then immediately hearing Connors' backstory from his own wife on his first day down there -- it would be easy to classify this as a stretch, but other than the coincidences that occur in comics all the time, the actual content remained riveting, and it was easy to see how a one-armed scientist would want to regrow an arm and introduce such healing ability in humans.&nbsp; I remained transfixed as Stan Lee and Steve Ditko guided me along the events, and through the entirety of the first 10 pages, I really had no reason to complain.\n[ASSET: center | TestingItOnHimself | width=67%]\n\nAnd let's be honest -- once you're read this story, who will <i>ever</i> forget the thrill on the face of Dr. Connors as he stares down at his newly-regrown right arm!&nbsp; To use his own words, it was, indeed, [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=\" the greatest medical feat of all time!\"]\n\nThe sad backstory of the Lizard is heart-breaking, especially the panel on page 11 that shows the Lizard scribbling down a warning to his family to flee the area as he feels the last bit of his humanity fading.&nbsp; And while I can understand Martha's reluctance to give up on her husband, delaying their exit from the edge of the swamp, the rest of page 11 that shows the young Billy out wandering around the swamp is [SPAN: severity=error; text=far less believable] (and even a bit dumb -- what mother wouldn't know <i>exactly</i> where her young son was during his nap?)\n\n\n\n\nIt's my opinion of course, but the Lizard has never been a favorite villain of mine, but even with this mild bias, the story seemed too formulaic for me, and I found myself not really invested.&nbsp; The Lizard planning to rule the world with the help of his giant lizard army who would all obey him seemed a bit far-fetched too.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nAh well...next issue we're promised the return of the Vulture!",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}